/// interpret.cdz — the minimal-kernel INTERPRET program, authored IN Cadenza (re-charter K0 repro).
///
/// The operator's minimal-kernel mandate (implementation/design/DESIGN-agent-runtime-minimal-kernel.md +
/// -broad-primitives.md): the Rust host understands NO events. A self-modifiable CADENZA program interprets
/// the event log and returns the host-ops to run; the tiny host just executes them. This is the FIRST such
/// program — the shape the daemon drives: `interpret : (log tail, new event) -> (List HostOp)`.
///
/// It is also the K0 repro v-effects asked for. v-effects verified (their probe MR 836493184) that a
/// `(List (Sum …))` PROVIDER RESULT already crosses to a Cadenza-peer consumer as an opaque runtime handle
/// via `extern_abi_val_type` — NO host-ABI widening needed for the RESULT direction. So the kernel's
/// interpret can return `(List HostOp)` today, as long as it compiles as a provider and its consumer is the
/// Cadenza executor peer. The still-to-verify bit is the compound ARG inbound (`List Event` INTO interpret —
/// v-peer-linking's arg-side); this module keeps the args SCALAR for now (a turn id + an event-kind tag) so
/// the RESULT path — the one we were blocked on — is gate-proven independent of the arg-side.
///
/// HostOp is the BROAD-primitive surface (forks 1+3 ruling): the ops are generic high-reach primitives
/// (exec / http / log-append), NOT a narrow agent API — Cadenza composes all behavior from these. Modeled
/// with scalar/String fields here (already-expressible across the boundary); the real payloads (Bytes) join
/// when v-peer-linking confirms the compound-arg inbound.

/// A host operation the interpret program asks the tiny kernel to run — the whole "syscall" surface, broad
/// and generic (operator forks 1+3: max reach per primitive, so no new host op is ever needed). Scalar/
/// String fields for now (the boundary-proven subset). `Append` = the one log write; `Exec` = run any host
/// command (the universal escape hatch); `Http` = any network call.
type HostOp =
  | Append(String)
  | Exec(String)
  | Http(String)
  | Noop

/// INTERPRET one landed event — the kernel's whole brain, in Cadenza. Given the new event's `kind` tag (a
/// scalar stand-in for the event; the real `(tail, event)` compound args land once v-peer-linking confirms
/// the inbound direction) and a `turn` id, decide the host-ops to run. This models the reactive core: a
/// "message" event (kind 1) → log an ack + exec a handler; a "tick" (kind 2) → an http poll; anything else
/// → do nothing. The RESULT is a `(List HostOp)` — the compound that crosses to the executor peer as a
/// handle (v-effects: already supported). Pure + total: same inputs → same op list (replayable).
def interpret(kind: Int64, turn: Int64) =
  if kind == 1 then
    [Append("ack"), Exec("handle-message")]
  else if kind == 2 then
    [Http("poll")]
  else
    [Noop]

/// The number of ops interpret schedules for a given event — a scalar the boundary can carry today, so the
/// suite gate-proves interpret's LOGIC (which ops fire per event kind) even before the full provider+peer
/// loop is wired. (`List.len` over the returned `(List HostOp)`.)
def op-count(kind: Int64, turn: Int64) = List.len(interpret(kind, turn))

@test def a_message_event_schedules_ack_then_handler() =
  op-count(1, 0) == 2

@test def a_tick_event_schedules_one_poll() =
  op-count(2, 0) == 1

@test def an_unknown_event_schedules_only_noop() =
  op-count(9, 0) == 1

/// The FIRST op for a message event is the log-append (ack) — pins the ORDER interpret emits (the executor
/// runs the list in order, so order is semantically load-bearing: ack before the handler runs).
@test def a_message_appends_before_it_execs() =
  match interpret(1, 0) with
  | [first, .. _] => (match first with
    | HostOp.Append(_) => true
    | _ => false)
  | [] => false
